Tracing the actual code: unstakeAndExitPool is not a member of the vault CurveConvex2Token at all — it is declared on
  the separate CurveConvexLib contract, which is deployed in the vault's constructor and stored as LP_LIB. The vault
  reaches it only through _delegateCall(LP_LIB, ...) from AbstractSingleSidedLP's internal redemption path, so the
  selector unstakeAndExitPool is not part of the vault's external ABI and a direct call to the vault address hits no
  such function (it reverts on dispatch). The only address at which unstakeAndExitPool is directly callable is the
  CurveConvexLib deployment itself, and calling it there executes in the library contract's own storage and, critically,
  under the library's own address(this). The Convex/gauge staked position and the Curve LP tokens are held by the vault
  (the delegatecall context), never by the library instance. So an unprivileged direct call to
  CurveConvexLib.unstakeAndExitPool(poolClaim, [0,0], ...) runs _unstakeLpTokens(poolClaim) →
  IConvexRewardPool.withdrawAndUnwrap(poolClaim, false) (or ICurveGauge.withdraw) against the library's zero staked
  balance, which reverts on underflow for any poolClaim > 0; with poolClaim == 0 nothing is unstaked and _exitPool burns
  zero LP, returning zeros. Either way the vault's position is untouched: there is no code path by which a call to the
  library address can move the vault's staked LP, because the reward-pool/gauge accounting is keyed on msg.sender, which
  is the library, not the vault.

  The claimed downstream consequences follow only from the premise that the vault's staked balance goes to zero, and
  that premise fails at the first step. super.convertToAssets never sees a drained position, honest redemptions do not
  revert, and there is no sandwichable forced exit of vault funds. The _minAmounts = [0,0] argument is only
  attacker-controlled on a call that can never move vault-owned LP; on the real path the min amounts are supplied by the
  vault's own redemption logic in AbstractSingleSidedLP, which is not shown here but is reached only through the
  vault's authenticated entry points.

  The finding is a delegatecall-library-mistaken-for-vault-entrypoint error: it attributes an external, unauthenticated
  helper on a stateless delegatecall target to the vault's ABI, and it assumes the helper operating on its own
  address(this) can dispatch the vault's Convex withdrawal. Neither holds. Additionally, even on the library address,
  transferYieldTokenToOwner and joinPoolAndStake are equally unauthenticated for the same architectural reason and are
  equally inert — the pattern is deliberate, and the absence of access control on the library is not exploitable absent
  a way to make the library's own token/stake balances non-zero, which nothing in the shown code provides (tokens
  transit the vault's context, and any stray balance would be a separate, non-High issue).

